#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e14129e-c8c9-5c9b-8ad4-feb9d618dd6b" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing Vertical Translations of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "0c18caa2-840d-5911-ade5-63fd59752b64" :question/math-academy-id "e-5306" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x} - 2$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}{\\color{red}\\,-\\,2}\n$$\n\nwe need to translate the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n*down* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nis translated down by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated down by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=-2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b1/b157913f6fca24190330cdfa5c795e069eff15de8a2079d9e29e1d6e41b95cb4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bd87ed4-1b63-516e-9945-5de0c15e0c90" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing Horizontal Translations of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "8ab32d98-1888-5ef0-95c1-81233cb7f449" :question/math-academy-id "e-5307" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x \\color{red} + 2}\n$$\n\nwe need to translate the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nto the *left* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nis translated to the left by $2$ units, the vertical asymptote $x = 0$ is also translated to the left by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new vertical asymptote is $x=-2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/e8/e8bff71235e67d8ad4046e94b5fe2682543674877a253ae6d4fe431504dbde3c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be4ff1c6-496e-5b3d-886f-5b6d21719469" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Horizontal and Vertical Translations of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5aee1873-5503-54ab-bf91-71a0abaf91a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5308" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-1}+2$." :question/worked-solution "The given function can be obtained by translating\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nboth horizontally and vertically.\n\nWe have two translations:\n\n- the $\\color{red}-1$ in the denominator means a horizontal translation of $1$ unit to the *right*, and\n- the $\\color{blue}+2$ outside the fraction means a vertical translation of $2$ units *up*.\n\nSo, the new graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fe/fea5a705657b22621f3eb5ffe40cfcd59aef11036674d47e6d1f72e301d531f3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be4ff1c6-496e-5b3d-886f-5b6d21719469" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Horizontal and Vertical Translations of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "937fe4c7-0995-5d6c-8cac-0529a74aed2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/ba1c28fb7fdf0ba5f772f1f6d6aa5be40967594b080db65ed4acd751f9152821.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178294} {:answer/id #uuid "9f81c84a-a0d7-519c-b949-4e7d11ca1bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/851b90f3d442a528ccc2441f0c15e3ef344be9ee54c6789b312673a5ee7ae06f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178295} {:answer/id #uuid "a79bb